        <technology>
            <technologyDescriptionAndIncludedProcesses>Several types of compounds and polymers are used as Flame Retardants (FR) for textile materials, including inorganic acids, acid salts and hydrates, organophosphorous and organo- bromine compounds, antimony salts/halogen systems etc. They may be classified into three categories:
(1) Primary FR based on phosphorous and/or halogen—the phosphorous derivatives act usu- ally in solid or condensed phase, while halogen (chlorine or bromine) is active in gaseous phase.
(2) FR using synergists such as nitrogen and antimony for phosphorous and halogen-based FRs respectively. Synergists themselves are not flame retardants. FRs exhibiting P/N and/ or Sb/X synergism are durable in nature.
(3) Adjunctive or physical FRs includes alumina trihydrate, boron compounds, silicates, and carbonates. Their activity is mainly physical, although recently some evidence of a chem- ical effect has been cited. They are nondurable and used only if durability in laundering is not important.

Phosphorous-based FRs have been in use for more than 150 years; the first patent was granted in 1735. Many such compounds are very effective for a wide range of fabrics and more so for cellulosics. They are easy to use, compatible with many textile chemicals and are favoured from both environmental and toxicological perspectives. Pekoflam DPN is a commercial organic phosphorous compound capable of providing a permanent flame-retardant finish on natural and regenerated cellulosic fibres. It is a pale, colourless liquid (pH 4.5–6.5), slightly cationic in acid medium. It is applied together with a catalyst, pref- erably phosphoric acid. It is recommended that a nitrogen-containing cross-linking agent, such as Arkofix NDS type (very low free formaldehyde on finished goods), should be used simul- taneously because a synergistic flame-retardancy effect is produced that withstands washing at the boil. It can be combined with wetting agents such as Sandozin grades or water and oil repellents such as Nuva grades. The amount of FR depends on the fibre type, fabric weight and construction and the desired flame retardancy. Light and open fabrics require a higher quantity than the one having a heavy and close weave.

In this process the finishing chemical is applied using the pad dry-cure method. The process includes the use of balloon padder, dryer, and stenter for curing. The process includes chemicals, energy, lube oil consumption and infrastructure. There is no direct emission to air since the emission comes from electricity usage, while hazardous waste comes from waste lube oil. In the model, the energy sources of machines used from electricity and steam. The consumption of lubricating oil using a thermobonding oven as a proxy. The GSM of the fabric is assumed to be 285 GSM cotton fabric



Choudhury, Asim Kumar Roy. 2017. Principle of textile finishing. United Kingdom: Elsevier. pp 201, 215, 217</technologyDescriptionAndIncludedProcesses>
